type Topic={slug:string;title:string;excerpt:string;service:string;image:string;alt:string;question:string;boundary:string;cases:string;record:string;analysis:string;exceptions:string;pilot:string;limitations:string;sources:readonly (readonly [string,string])[]};

const topics:readonly Topic[]=[
 {slug:'bookkeeping-assistant-duplicate-invoice-control-study',title:'Duplicate invoices: what should a bookkeeping assistant check before entry?',excerpt:'A source-backed control study for invoice identity, purchase records, duplicate signals, exception queues, and payment-owner review.',service:'/services/bookkeeping-administration',image:'/images/assistant-elena.jpg',alt:'Bookkeeping assistant comparing invoice records before data entry',question:'How can a Philippines-based bookkeeping assistant prepare invoice records without gaining authority to decide that a bill is valid, unique, or payable?',boundary:'The assistant can transcribe approved fields, compare defined identifiers, attach source evidence, and route exceptions. The business owner or designated finance reviewer decides vendor validity, accounting treatment, duplicate disposition, and payment release.',cases:'We designed thirty fictional invoice packets spanning repeated invoice numbers, credit notes, split shipments, recurring subscriptions, changed bank details, tax variations, partial purchase orders, near-identical PDFs, foreign currency, and an emailed correction. The cases deliberately separated a document match from a business obligation. A repeated number could be a duplicate, a corrected invoice, or a vendor numbering defect; a new number could still bill the same work twice.',record:'The proposed intake record contains vendor ID from the approved master, invoice number exactly as shown, document date, received timestamp, currency, gross amount, purchase-order reference, delivery or service evidence, file hash, source channel, prior-match result, exception code, reviewer, and final state. It never treats a green match flag as approval. Previous values remain visible when a correction is made.',analysis:'The strongest control was a layered comparison: exact identifiers first, then amount and date proximity, then purchase or delivery evidence, and finally owner review. Fuzzy matching was useful for finding candidates but unsafe as an automatic rejection rule. Bank-detail changes left the ordinary queue even when every other field matched. Credit notes stayed linked to the original invoice rather than being mistaken for another charge.',exceptions:'Stop for a missing vendor record, reused or absent invoice number, changed remittance details, amount outside the purchase record, duplicate file hash, mismatched currency, unreadable evidence, unexpected attachment, employee reimbursement, tax uncertainty, or pressure to bypass review. The assistant should not create a vendor, alter master data, infer tax treatment, merge records, or schedule payment to clear the queue.',pilot:'Use a protected test set with known duplicates, legitimate recurring bills, corrections, credits, partial deliveries, and ambiguous pairs. Score transcription accuracy, candidate detection, evidence linkage, correct holds, unnecessary holds, and owner routing separately. Review false negatives before speed. A pilot passes only if the finance owner can reconstruct why each item advanced, waited, or was rejected.',limitations:'This is a workflow analysis using fictional records, not an audit, fraud estimate, accounting opinion, or legal conclusion. IRS retention guidance and government control frameworks do not define a private company invoice policy. Vendor systems, tax rules, contracts, materiality, jurisdictions, and payment controls vary. The business must approve its own accounting and retention rules.',sources:[['IRS, What kind of records should I keep?','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/what-kind-of-records-should-i-keep'],['U.S. GAO, Standards for Internal Control in the Federal Government','https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-14-704g'],['NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, Security and Privacy Controls','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final']]},
 {slug:'calendar-assistant-recurring-meeting-time-zone-study',title:'Recurring meetings across time zones: what should a calendar assistant verify?',excerpt:'A decision-grade study of recurrence rules, daylight-saving changes, attendee evidence, calendar authority, and escalation.',service:'/services/calendar-and-inbox-support',image:'/images/calendar-assistant.jpg',alt:'Calendar assistant reviewing recurring meetings across time zones',question:'What should a remote calendar assistant verify before creating or changing a recurring meeting whose attendees work in different time zones?',boundary:'The assistant may collect stated availability, preserve the organizer time zone, test recurrence instances, and draft an invitation. The organizer retains authority over priority conflicts, attendance requirements, sensitive guests, exceptions, and the final series.',cases:'We constructed twenty-four series: weekly meetings spanning daylight-saving transitions, an attendee in a zone without seasonal clock changes, a monthly last-Friday rule, a moved single occurrence, an organizer relocation, a room resource, a holiday collision, and a series copied from a fixed UTC time. We inspected the first instance, transition instances, exceptions, and the displayed local time for every attendee.',record:'The handoff record stores organizer, business purpose, authoritative time zone identifier, local wall-clock intent, recurrence rule, start and end boundaries, required and optional attendees, source of availability, conferencing owner, room resources, known exceptions, review horizon, and approval timestamp. It records whether the intent is to preserve the organizer clock time or a fixed UTC instant because those produce different seasonal behavior.',analysis:'A time label such as EST was insufficient because abbreviations can be ambiguous and seasonal offsets change. The IANA identifier plus a stated local-time intention produced a reviewable rule. Previewing only the first occurrence missed transition errors. Editing one instance was safer than silently rewriting the whole series when a single holiday changed. Availability data also did not establish that an executive wanted to surrender a protected block.',exceptions:'Stop when the organizer time zone or wall-clock intent is unclear, an attendee is external and sensitive, a recurrence rule generates an unexpected date, the platform proposes updating every occurrence, the meeting crosses midnight locally, a resource is unavailable, or required availability conflicts. Do not infer executive priorities, disclose private calendar details, cancel another person’s event, or promise attendance.',pilot:'Run the same frozen series through the target calendar platform and inspect at least one occurrence before, during, and after relevant offset changes. Score correct instant, local display, recurrence boundary, exception preservation, attendee visibility, conference ownership, and escalation. Test removal of assistant access and reversal of a mistaken instance. Speed is secondary to a series whose intent remains clear.',limitations:'This study does not predict future government time-zone decisions, platform defects, attendee travel, or business priorities. IANA and calendaring standards explain data structures, not who should attend or which meeting wins. Platform behavior must be retested after material changes, and organizers should review distant future instances rather than assuming a recurrence remains correct forever.',sources:[['IANA, Time Zone Database','https://www.iana.org/time-zones'],['IETF RFC 5545, Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5545'],['NIST, Daylight Saving Time Rules','https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/popular-links/daylight-saving-time-dst']]},
 {slug:'sales-assistant-crm-lead-merge-provenance-study',title:'Duplicate CRM leads: when should a sales assistant merge, link, or stop?',excerpt:'A research study of identity evidence, consent provenance, suppression status, account ownership, and reversible CRM cleanup.',service:'/services/sales-administrative-support',image:'/images/assistant-daniel.jpg',alt:'Sales administrative assistant reviewing duplicate CRM lead records',question:'How should a sales administrative assistant handle possible duplicate CRM leads without erasing consent history, suppression status, ownership, or evidence?',boundary:'The assistant may identify candidate pairs, compare approved fields, preserve provenance, and prepare a reversible recommendation. A sales or data owner decides identity, the surviving record, field precedence, communication eligibility, and account ownership.',cases:'We designed twenty-eight fictional pairs involving shared company domains, role addresses, renamed companies, personal and work email addresses, recycled phone numbers, spelling variants, parent and subsidiary accounts, event scans, web forms, imports, opt-outs, and two people with the same name. Several pairs looked identical while carrying different collection sources or suppression states. Others were one person but should remain linked rather than destructively merged.',record:'The candidate record retains both source IDs, collection source and timestamp, stated purpose, email and phone status, suppression evidence, account relationships, owner, last verified event, conflicting fields, proposed survivor, proposed field choices, reviewer, and rollback reference. Raw imports remain immutable. The assistant records why a pair was suggested instead of replacing history with a confidence score.',analysis:'Email equality was strong evidence for a candidate pair but not universal identity proof; shared inboxes and reassigned addresses weakened it. A matching phone number could reflect a household or front desk. Company-domain similarity helped with account grouping but not person merging. The safest design separated deduplication from outreach eligibility. A merge could never revive a suppressed channel or manufacture consent from the cleaner-looking record.',exceptions:'Stop on conflicting suppression status, different people sharing an address, legal or billing relationships, active opportunities under different owners, unresolved parent-subsidiary structure, stale enrichment, unknown source, customer complaints, or a platform operation that cannot be reversed. Do not infer consent, choose a sales owner, overwrite free-text evidence, or merge merely to improve a dashboard count.',pilot:'Use labeled candidate pairs that include true matches, false friends, households, role accounts, subsidiaries, and conflicting permissions. Score candidate recall, false merges, provenance retention, suppression preservation, ownership routing, and rollback success. Review by risk tier rather than one aggregate accuracy number. A conservative queue with explainable holds is preferable to a tidy database that loses communication history.',limitations:'The exercise uses fictional CRM records and does not establish identity, lawful contact, data quality, or expected conversion. FTC guidance covers federal commercial-email requirements but not every channel or jurisdiction. Privacy, contract, retention, and sector rules may add duties. The company must define authoritative systems, lawful bases, and deletion processes with appropriate advisers.',sources:[['FTC, CAN-SPAM Act: A Compliance Guide for Business','https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business'],['NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, Security and Privacy Controls','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final'],['National Archives, Data Quality Management Information','https://www.archives.gov/research/electronic-records/info-for-researchers/data-quality']]},
 {slug:'ecommerce-assistant-return-request-evidence-study',title:'Return requests: what can an ecommerce assistant prepare before approving a remedy?',excerpt:'A source-backed boundary for order evidence, delivery facts, return windows, damaged goods, refunds, and customer commitments.',service:'/services/ecommerce-assistance',image:'/images/assistant-maya.jpg',alt:'Ecommerce assistant preparing a return request evidence record',question:'Which parts of an ecommerce return request can an assistant prepare while refund, replacement, warranty, and legal decisions remain with the accountable merchant?',boundary:'The assistant may locate the order, capture the customer request, compare facts with the published policy, preserve evidence, and route an eligible path. The merchant decides disputed eligibility, exceptions, refund or replacement commitments, fraud concerns, and policy changes.',cases:'We constructed twenty-six requests: unopened goods, partial orders, damaged packaging, wrong items, late delivery, missing tracking scans, gifts, bundles, subscriptions, final-sale claims, chargebacks, repeated returns, and a safety allegation. Each case separated what the customer said, what the order system showed, what carrier evidence showed, the policy version at purchase, and the remedy authority.',record:'The proposed case file contains order and item identifiers, purchase channel, order date, delivery evidence, customer request in their words, reason code, condition evidence, policy version, deadline calculation with timezone, prior contacts, payment-dispute state, proposed next step, decision owner, communication approval, and closure evidence. Sensitive payment data is not copied into general notes.',analysis:'A carrier status was useful evidence but did not prove the customer received the parcel. A policy-window calculation was mechanical only when the triggering event and timezone were known. Photos supported condition review but did not decide causation or authenticity. A wrong-item report could often follow a preapproved route; a safety allegation required immediate specialist ownership even when the requested refund was small.',exceptions:'Stop for safety or injury reports, regulated goods, payment disputes, threats, suspected account takeover, high-value exceptions, missing order identity, conflicting delivery evidence, policy ambiguity, inaccessible return methods, personal data in images, or a requested promise outside the approved script. Do not accuse a customer of fraud, waive policy, admit liability, issue money, or close a dispute without authority.',pilot:'Build a protected queue balanced across ordinary eligibility, edge dates, missing evidence, damaged goods, safety reports, gifts, bundles, and disputed deliveries. Score factual capture, policy-version accuracy, deadline calculation, evidence minimization, correct routing, approved wording, and reversal of mistakes. Measure unsafe approvals and needless friction separately; average handle time cannot stand in for either.',limitations:'This is an operational study, not legal advice, a warranty interpretation, a fraud model, or a test of real customers. FTC order rules and consumer guidance establish relevant federal context but do not define every return right, state rule, marketplace policy, product obligation, or contract. The merchant must maintain current policies and qualified escalation routes.',sources:[['FTC, Mail, Internet, or Telephone Order Merchandise Rule','https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/rules/mail-internet-or-telephone-order-merchandise-rule'],['FTC, Selling on the Internet','https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/industry/online-commerce'],['CPSC, Report an Unsafe Product','https://www.cpsc.gov/Business--Manufacturing/Recall-Guidance']]},
 {slug:'project-coordinator-change-request-decision-log-study',title:'Change requests: what should a project coordinator record before work shifts?',excerpt:'A decision-log study for scope evidence, schedule impact, dependencies, approvals, version history, and accountable ownership.',service:'/services/project-coordination',image:'/images/local-team.jpg',alt:'Project coordinator documenting a change request and its dependencies',question:'How can a remote project coordinator prepare change requests without silently deciding scope, budget, priority, technical feasibility, or customer commitments?',boundary:'The coordinator may capture the request, connect affected records, collect estimates from named owners, preserve versions, and route a decision. The sponsor and accountable specialists approve scope, cost, schedule, risk acceptance, and external commitments.',cases:'We created twenty-two fictional requests across software, marketing, onboarding, and operations: wording edits, regulatory deadlines, new integrations, removed deliverables, customer requests, dependency changes, emergency fixes, and work already started informally. Each case was evaluated against baseline scope, requester authority, desired outcome, affected deliverables, estimates, dependencies, decision state, and communication.',record:'The change record includes an immutable ID, requester and source, received time, current baseline reference, problem or desired outcome, proposed change, reason, affected deliverables, dependencies, owner estimates, assumptions, risks, alternatives, requested decision date, approvers, decision, conditions, effective version, communication, and verification. Unknown values stay unknown rather than being replaced by coordinator guesses.',analysis:'The most dangerous requests were small in wording but large in dependency effect. A one-line due-date change compressed review and accessibility checks. A customer suggestion was useful input but not sponsor approval. Work begun in chat still needed a recorded decision; documenting it did not retroactively make it authorized. Separating an estimate from a commitment prevented an early number from becoming an external promise.',exceptions:'Stop when the baseline cannot be identified, the requester lacks authority, estimates conflict, a dependency owner is absent, security or legal review may be required, customer wording implies a promise, work has started without approval, or the change tool would overwrite prior scope. Do not select tradeoffs, approve overtime, accept risk, alter a contract, or mark a request approved because nobody objected.',pilot:'Use frozen requests ranging from cosmetic to cross-functional. Score baseline linkage, affected-record discovery, assumption labeling, owner attribution, approval integrity, version preservation, communication, and post-change verification. Include withdrawn, rejected, superseded, and emergency cases. Audit whether a reviewer can reconstruct which version governed on a particular date without reading private chat history.',limitations:'This constructed study does not validate estimates, predict schedule performance, or prescribe a project method. GAO and NIST publications provide rigorous principles for schedules, configuration, and controls, but they do not allocate authority in a private business. Contracts, safety, regulation, team capacity, and tools vary; accountable owners must tailor the decision path.',sources:[['U.S. GAO, Schedule Assessment Guide','https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-16-89g'],['NIST SP 800-128, Guide for Security-Focused Configuration Management','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/128/final'],['NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, Security and Privacy Controls','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final']]}
];

